  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Corecivic has $399M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, spread across 42 awards with an average award size of $9.5M. Corecivic obligations are up 0.9% year over year. Corecivic received $318M from the Department of Homeland Security and $81.7M from the Department of Justice in the current fiscal year to date. Corecivic revenue is concentrated in Security Guards and Patrol Services, which accounts for $336M, followed by Other Justice, Public Order, and Safety Activities at $33.3M and Other Residential Care Facilities at $30.4M. Corecivic obligations have risen from $396M in FY25 to $399M in FY26. Corecivic figures in prior years include $272M in FY21, $288M in FY22, $253M in FY23, and $271M in FY24. Corecivic award-count and market mix indicate that large, recurring DHS programs are driving the current-year total. Corecivic averages and the top-agency split show heavy dependency on a small number of high-dollar awards and a primary market in security and detention-related services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big are Corecivic's FY2026 federal obligations?",
        "body": "Corecivic has $399M in obligations FY2026 to date across 42 awards. Corecivic average award size is $9.5M. Corecivic obligations are up 0.9% year over year compared with FY25 obligations of $396M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where does Corecivic get most of its federal revenue?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security is Corecivic's largest buyer at $318M. The Department of Justice is Corecivic's second-largest buyer at $81.7M. Corecivic markets are led by Security Guards and Patrol Services at $336M, with Other Justice, Public Order, and Safety Activities at $33.3M and Other Residential Care Facilities at $30.4M."
      }
    ]
  },
